Start here
==========

This is a Windows port of litmus (https://github.com/notroj/litmus).
If you are picking this up cold, read these in order:

  1. README.md    what this fork is, how to build it, how to run it
  2. AGENTS.md    how to use litmus to test a WebDAV server, and how to
                  read the results; also the JSON schema
  3. PATCHES.md   what was changed inside neon/ and why, needed before
                  you update the bundled neon
  4. This file    what is left to do

Layout:

  src/            the test suites, one .c per suite, plus common.c,
                  the suite registry (suites.h, litmus-cli.c), the
                  benchmark (bench.c) and the harness self-test
                  (selftest.c)
  neon/           vendored neon 0.37.1, checked in, not a submodule
  neon/test/common/tests.c
                  the shared harness: result formatting, counters,
                  JSON output, run_suite(). Patched; see PATCHES.md
  win32/config.h  generated config.h, checked in so Makefile.w32 works
                  without autotools
  VERSION         the fork version, read by both build paths
  tests/          test harnesses and expected results; see below
  Makefile.w32    the no-autotools build
  .github/        CI: lint, both build paths, three gates, release

Everything is one executable, litmus-cli, with the suite as a
subcommand:

  litmus-cli basic http://host/dav/
  litmus-cli locks --json http://host/dav/
  litmus-cli all http://host/dav/
  litmus-cli bench http://host/dav/
  litmus-cli selftest
  litmus-cli list

Test harnesses in tests/:

  harness.sh      runs litmus-cli selftest in all three output modes
                  and compares against tests/harness-expected.  No
                  server, no network, under a second.  Run it first.
  wsgidav.sh      runs the suites against a local wsgidav; --check
                  compares against expected-wsgidav.txt.  This is the
                  gate: it must print "All suites match the expected
                  results" before and after every change.
  godav.sh        runs them against golang.org/x/net/webdav instead;
                  --check compares against expected-godav.txt.  Needed
                  because wsgidav cannot exercise the lock tests at
                  all (see below).
  capture.sh      captures every suite in every output mode, for the
                  byte-for-byte regression check
  compare-captures.sh
                  diffs two capture directories
  hyperfine.sh    times litmus itself rather than the server, against
                  an empty main() as the control
  python.sh       sourced by the two server harnesses to find a Python
  httpd.sh        upstream's Apache-in-a-container harness, Unix only

Neither test server is complete, and the difference matters:

  * wsgidav 4.3.5 supports dead properties, so the props suite is
    meaningful against it, but it answers LOCK with the Content-Type
    "application; charset=utf-8", which is not a media type and not an
    XML one.  neon discards the body unparsed, litmus never sees the
    lock token, and everything downstream of holding a lock is
    skipped.  Any change to the lock tests is invisible there.
  * x/net/webdav locks correctly, including the RFC 4918 section 7.3
    treatment of a LOCK on an unmapped URL, but has no dead property
    store, so four props tests fail against it.

So: verify lock work against godav.sh, property work against
wsgidav.sh, and do not read either result as the whole picture.

Before you change anything
==========================

Run the gates and note the numbers:

  ./tests/harness.sh
  ./tests/wsgidav.sh --check
  ./tests/godav.sh --check

All three must pass.  If they do not, fix that before doing anything
else.  Everything below assumes a green baseline.

Conventions, in code and in prose:

  * Binary units, always: KiB, MiB, GiB, powers of 1024. Never KB, MB
    or GB for a quantity of bytes, and never a decimal power of ten.
    Rates are MiB/s.
  * Timestamps are ISO 8601 in UTC with millisecond precision and a
    trailing Z, e.g. 2026-08-18T13:04:54.429Z. Truncate sub-second
    digits, do not round, so a stamp never names a moment that had not
    happened yet.
  * Durations are seconds to millisecond resolution, and are
    wall-clock. Say so when reporting them, because they include server
    and network time.
  * Quote exact byte counts where the exact figure matters, with the
    rounded binary figure in brackets after it.

If you touch neon/test/common/tests.c or src/common.c, the default text
output must stay byte for byte identical. Prove it:

  make -f Makefile.w32 && ./tests/capture.sh /tmp/before
  ... make the change ...
  make -f Makefile.w32 && ./tests/capture.sh /tmp/after
  ./tests/compare-captures.sh /tmp/before /tmp/after

Do not skip this. It is the only thing standing between this fork and
silently breaking every script that parses litmus output.

One caveat, found the hard way: two captures of the same build are not
always identical.  Against wsgidav on Windows, copymove occasionally
sees a transient 403 or 412 from the server on a COPY or on the DELETE
before it, which surfaces as copy_overwrite failing or copy_abspath
warning.  Fifteen consecutive copymove runs against a dedicated server
were identical, so this is the server's filesystem under load rather
than anything in litmus.  If a diff is confined to copymove, capture a
third time before believing it.  Everything else is deterministic.

High value work
===============

Nothing.  The list that used to be here is done:

  * The docs were rewritten for the single executable.
  * CI gained a lint gate, a concurrency group, dependabot, caching,
    the godav gate, and a release job that publishes the executable
    under a fixed URL and refuses to overwrite a good release.
  * The start-up floor was measured and left alone; see below.
  * --json gained the "error" object.
  * The harness gained a self-test that needs no server.

Cutting a release
=================

Bump VERSION, commit, wait for CI to go green on that commit, then

  git tag -a vX.Y-winN
  git push origin vX.Y-winN

The tag must be annotated and its name must be "v" plus VERSION
exactly, or the release job refuses to run.  It also refuses if a
release with that tag already exists, and it checks both before it
uploads anything, so a good release cannot be overwritten by accident.
The workflow copies the tag's message above the generated notes, so the
tag message is the release description.

Do not quote the built executable's size in a tag message: it is not
known until CI has built it, and it moves with the OpenSSL and expat
versions the runner happens to have.  v0.18-win2 was tagged with the
size measured on a development machine, 8610579 bytes, where the
published binary is 8593710.

Afterwards, confirm the fixed URL resolves and the binary runs:

  curl -fsSL -o litmus-cli.exe https://github.com/Azathothas/litmus-windows/releases/latest/download/litmus-cli.exe
  ./litmus-cli.exe selftest

Outstanding work
================

Smaller maintenance items, in rough priority order.

* neon's ne_lock() reports "Response missing activelock for %s" with
  the token that is, by definition, missing, so it always prints
  "(null)".  Passing NULL to %s is undefined behaviour; mingw and glibc
  both happen to print "(null)".  The message would be far more useful
  with the lock path in it.  Not fixed, because it is part of the
  default text output that this fork promises not to change, and it is
  upstream neon's message rather than litmus's.  Worth reporting to
  neon.

* The autotools build's remaining EXEEXT problem is fixed and the
  relink-every-run problem with it, but only for the one executable
  that is left. If another program is ever added, put $(EXEEXT)
  through its rules too and keep `subdirs' an order-only prerequisite.

* Test failure paths leak. Most tests build URLs with ne_concat and
  return through the ON* macros, which do not free anything. The cases
  that left files on disk have been fixed, and lockbomb's threadfn()
  now always destroys its session and hands back a message the caller
  frees. The remaining leaks are memory only, in a process that is
  about to exit, so they are untidy rather than harmful. Cleaning them
  up properly means a cleanup path per test.

* Nothing reports which suites a server actually supports before
  running them, so `litmus-cli all' against a class 1 server spends its
  time skipping. Not important.

Things that were looked at and deliberately not done
====================================================

* Cutting the start-up floor.  Twenty runs each against a local Go
  server: an empty main() built with the same compiler took 18.8 ms
  +/- 3.1 ms, `litmus-cli list' 22.4 ms, `litmus-cli version' 23.8 ms,
  the four-test http suite 27.3 ms, and the seventeen-test basic suite
  39.4 ms.  Everything litmus does before its first byte of output
  costs under 5 ms against a floor that belongs to Windows process
  creation, and printing usage, printing the version and listing the
  suites all measure the same to within a standard deviation, so there
  is no code path to blame.  What is left is image size, paged in at
  load.  tests/hyperfine.sh runs the control first so the comparison
  is in front of you.

* A chunked-upload suite.  Several widely deployed servers implement
  the ownCloud/Nextcloud extension: MKCOL a per-transfer directory,
  PUT the parts into it under zero-padded numeric names, then MOVE a
  virtual assembly path onto the destination.  Not done, for two
  reasons.  It is vendor-specific rather than RFC 4918, so the URL
  layout is a property of the server and the suite would need
  per-server configuration to be usable at all.  More decisively,
  neither test server implements it, so the suite could not be
  verified against anything -- and shipping an unverified suite is
  exactly what the rest of this tree is set up to avoid.  It needs a
  third test server first.

* Merging the tolsen fork's tests wholesale, requested in upstream
  issue 8. It is 1643 lines with no shared history, stale by many
  commits, and unreviewed. Individual test cases were lifted from it
  after being read and verified on their own merits: the escaped-space
  collection test, and the MKCOL 201 and DELETE 204 status warnings.
  The rest was either unsound (a MKCOL of a URI
  with a literal space, which is not a valid request target) or
  dependent on a particular server's authentication setup.

* Upstream issue 21 asks for PROPPATCH tests to accept a 207 without
  looking inside it, on the grounds that arbitrary dead properties need
  not be allowed. The first half of that is now implemented properly:
  litmus_proppatch() reports the status from inside the multistatus, so
  a server that refuses with 207+423 is judged on the 423. The second
  half, being lenient about servers with no dead property support at
  all, was rejected upstream with sound reasoning and is not
  implemented here either: the property tests would be pointless.

* Upstream issue 22 (fail_cond_put_unlocked) was analysed upstream and
  litmus's behaviour is correct. Nothing to do.

* Connection reuse was measured, in case it was a performance problem.
  It is not: a props run makes 36 requests over one TCP connection. The
  slowest tests are copy_coll and move_coll in copymove, at about two
  seconds each, and that time is the server copying a collection, not
  litmus. There is nothing to win on the client side.

* Tracing the lockbomb worker threads and the benchmark transfers.
  They create their own sessions rather than using i_session, so the
  --trace hooks are never attached. This is deliberate: writing to one
  FILE from twenty threads would need locking, tracing 400,000
  lock/unlock round trips would not be useful, and dumping the
  benchmark's bodies would dominate the measurement it is trying to
  make. Said so in README.md and AGENTS.md.

* Pinning the HTTP protocol version for the benchmark, which is what
  you would want against a server that behaves differently over
  HTTP/2. neon 0.37.1 speaks HTTP/1.1 only, so it cannot be done here;
  it would need a different HTTP client. Said so in README.md.

* Prefixing message bodies in the --trace output the way request and
  response headers are prefixed. Bodies are dumped by neon inside
  square brackets instead. That is better, not worse: PROPFIND and LOCK
  bodies are XML, so nearly every line starts with "<", which is also
  the response-header prefix. The brackets keep the boundary
  unambiguous.
